{"name":"Plush Suede","hex":"#b1928c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"plush","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Very extravagant. (Can we add an example for this sense?)","Very expensive, or appearing expensive; opulent, luxurious."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A textile fabric with a nap or shag on one side, longer and softer than the nap of velvet.","A plush toy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/plush"},{"term":"suede","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of soft leather, made from calfskin, with a brushed texture to resemble fabric, often used to make boots, clothing and fashion accessories."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of suede."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/suede"}],"entries":[{"title":"Stuffed toy","blocks":[{"text":"A stuffed toy is a toy with an outer fabric sewn from a textile and stuffed with flexible material. They are known by many names, such as stuffed animals, plush toys, plushies, stuffies and teddies; in Britain and Australia, they may also be called soft toys or cuddly toys. Stuffed toys are made in many different forms, but most resemble real animals, mythological creatures, cartoon characters, or inanimate objects. They can be commercially or home-produced from numerous materials, most commonly pile textiles like plush for the outer material and synthetic fiber for the stuffing. Often designed for children, some stuffed toys have become fads and collector's items."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Nachbildung_55PB_Steiff_Museum_Giengen.jpg/330px-Nachbildung_55PB_Steiff_Museum_Giengen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stuffed_toy"},{"title":"Suede","blocks":[{"text":"Suede is a type of leather with a fuzzy, napped finish, commonly used for jackets, shoes, fabrics, purses, furniture, and other items."}],"image":"https://thumb.